# Rank 1391 / CVE-2026-27904 (GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74, CWE-1333):
# isaacs/minimatch compiles a glob pattern to a JavaScript RegExp. Extended-glob
# groups -- `*(a|b)`, `+(a|b)`, `?(a|b)`, `@(a|b)`, `!(a|b)`, the ksh/bash
# `extglob` syntax minimatch documents and gates behind its `noext` option --
# each become one quantified alternation in the generated source: `AST.type` '*'
# closes with `)*`, '+' with `)+', '?' with `)?`. The parser that builds them,
# `AST.#parseAST` in src/ast.ts, is a recursive descent that calls itself once per
# group, and in the vulnerable revision the recursion has no ceiling at all --
# the only bound is the length of the pattern string:
#
#   static #parseAST(str: string, ast: AST, pos: number, opt: MinimatchOptions): number {
#     ...
#     if (!opt.noext && isExtglobType(c) && str.charAt(i) === '(') {
#       ast.push(acc); acc = ''
#       const ext = new AST(c, ast)
#       i = AST.#parseAST(str, ext, i, opt)        // <-- no depth argument
#       ast.push(ext)
#       continue
#     }
#     ...
#     if (isExtglobType(c) && str.charAt(i) === '(') {
#       part.push(acc); acc = ''
#       const ext = new AST(c, part)
#       part.push(ext)
#       i = AST.#parseAST(str, ext, i, opt)        // <-- nor here
#       continue
#     }
#
# Nesting the groups nests the quantifiers, and nested unbounded quantification
# over overlapping alternatives is the textbook catastrophic-backtracking shape.
# `*(*(*(*(*(*(a|f)|g)|h)|i)|j)|k)` is 31 characters and compiles to six nested
# `(?:...)*` groups; the regression test the fixed revision ships asserts that matching
# `'a'.repeat(15) + 'z'` against it completes in under 100ms, which the
# vulnerable revision does not. The pattern is the first argument of the
# documented entry points -- `minimatch(path, pattern)`, `new Minimatch(pattern)`,
# `AST.fromGlob(pattern)` -- so any caller that matches against a
# caller-supplied or config-supplied glob is exposed. The weakness is not a bad
# regex literal and not a match-semantics error: no regex in the repository is
# wrong, and no path escapes an exclusion. It is an input-driven expansion whose
# cost grows with a nesting depth nothing bounds.
#
# 11d0df6165d15a955462316b26d52e5efae06fce ("limit nested extglob recursion,
# flatten extglobs") fixes it in two halves. The recursion gains a depth
# parameter compared against a configured ceiling, so past the limit the extglob
# characters are simply not parsed and that sub-pattern falls back to
# `noextglob: true` behaviour:
#
#   static #parseAST(str, ast, pos, opt, extDepth): number {
#     const maxDepth = opt.maxExtglobRecursion ?? 2
#     ...
#     const doRecurse = !opt.noext && isExtglobType(c) && str.charAt(i) === '(' && extDepth <= maxDepth
#     if (doRecurse) { ... i = AST.#parseAST(str, ext, i, opt, extDepth + 1) ... }
#
# and `#flatten`/`adopt` coalesce nested groups whose types compose --
# `*(a|@(b|c)|d)` becomes `*(a|b|c|d)` -- via an `adoptionMap` recording which
# outer type may absorb which inner type, so the common deep-but-harmless pattern
# never reaches the new ceiling. src/index.ts documents `maxExtglobRecursion`
# alongside the existing `maxGlobstarRecursion`, default 2.
#
# The pool row is correct: repository, CVE and commit all resolve, the metadata
# flags check out, and the vulnerable `#parseAST` with no depth
# parameter is present at the parent, c3448c4. The recorded CWE-1333 is right.
# No corpus correction.
#
# What fires without this. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB`
# over the vulnerable checkout reported **zero findings** on the whole tree, so
# nothing to reuse and nothing to suppress. `vyql match` labels 75 nodes in
# src/, including `code.RegexCompileSink` on the `new RegExp(re, flags)` at
# ast.ts:400 -- but the fixed revision leaves that call byte-identical, so the ReDoS rule
# VYQL-DOS-001 (`core.UserControlledData -> code.RegexCompileSink`) could not
# discriminate the revisions even with a library parameter as a taint source.
# `code.CatastrophicRegex` reads a regex *literal's* structure and there is no
# such literal here: the dangerous pattern is assembled at run time from the
# caller's glob. Rank 1343 measured the regex-literal-shape route unshippable
# anyway (1 real in 7-11 across 268 literals).
#
# Rank 1381's `module.exports = class X` gap does not bite: minimatch is
# TypeScript with a real `export class AST`, and `vyql query -type Param`
# reports `AST.#parseAST#param#str|ast|pos|opt`, while `vyql query -call
# parseAST` resolves all three call sites with `path=AST.#parseAST`.
#
# Self-recursion itself is still not expressible, which is why the binding does
# not key on it. Both `function_name:` and `call_path:` reach a JavaScript
# function-scope presence node -- `node.context.functionName contains "parseAST"`
# matches ast.ts:245 alone -- but a binding cannot unify two facts on one node,
# so `node.context.callPath contains "AST.#parseAST"` matches ast.ts:245 (the
# recursion) *and* ast.ts:371 (`fromGlob`, an ordinary caller) alike. That is the
# JavaScript instance of the missing self-recursion/depth-cap fact rank 1320
# recorded for C. The binding therefore keys on what the library documents
# instead: the read of the `noext` extglob switch, which in the whole of minimatch
# occurs exactly once and exactly at the recursion decision.
#
# code.NestedExtglobExpansionWithoutDepthLimit is minted rather than reused.
# code.CatastrophicRegex and code.UnboundedEmailRegexValidation are the two
# existing ReDoS-class review concepts and both judge a pattern's own text;
# code.UnboundedAllocationReview reads "allocation sizes derived from parsed
# counts", which this is not; code.GsonObjectTypeAdapterRecursiveRead is the same
# weakness shape (recursive parse, no depth limit) welded to one Java API.
# VYQL-DOS-047 is minted above the DOS-045 ceiling, skipping the released
# DOS-046.
#
# Measured, `--cache off`, union over three runs. The A/B is `src/` at the fixed
# commit and at its parent: **1 flag on the vulnerable revision (src/ast.ts:245),
# 0 on the patched revision**, and each negation half discriminates on its own --
# dropping the selector list still gives 1/0, dropping the identifier list still
# gives 1/0. The reachable set is enumerated rather than sampled, because a
# five-file library gives a vacuous zero: across every JavaScript/TypeScript tree
# cloned for this campaign a member read of `.noext`/`.noextglob` appears in
# exactly five files outside minimatch, all vendored glob matchers. Two of them
# (`browser-es-module-loader/dist/babel-worker.js` in libvncserver's noVNC
# vendor tree, and the identical `f5_babel-worker.js` twice over) bundle
# minimatch 3.x, whose `parse()` builds the same nested `plTypes` quantifiers,
# caps pattern *length* at 64KB and nesting depth not at all: 2 flags each, one
# on `parse` and one on the browserify module wrapper that encloses it, since a
# JavaScript function context inherits its nested functions' tokens. Those are
# true positives on an end-of-life copy. The other three are yarn 4.0.2 and
# 4.6.0 releases, which bundle picomatch: 0 flags, because picomatch parses
# extglobs iteratively against a paren stack instead of recursing, and it has no
# nesting ceiling either -- an honest false negative of the same weakness in a
# different implementation, not a suppression. Full scans of lodash, marked,
# markdown-it, mathjs and graphql-js report 0 of this concept and no change in
# their totals; none contains `noext`, so that zero is corroboration, not
# evidence.
#
# The three cases below differ only in the ceiling: absent, read from the
# documented option, and held in a differently-named local -- the third is there
# because a negation that only knows `maxExtglobRecursion` would be this fix's
# own identifier rather than the remediation.

test "rank1391: an extglob parser recurses into a nested group with no ceiling on nesting depth"
  lang javascript
  file src/ast.ts
  expect VYQL-DOS-047
  expect_review code.NestedExtglobExpansionWithoutDepthLimit
  code
  ```
  const types = new Set(['!', '?', '+', '*', '@']);
  const isExtglobType = (c) => types.has(c);

  export class AST {
    constructor(type, parent, options = {}) {
      this.type = type;
      this.parts = [];
      this.parent = parent;
    }

    push(...parts) {
      for (const p of parts) {
        if (p === '') continue;
        this.parts.push(p);
      }
    }

    static parseAST(str, ast, pos, opt) {
      let escaping = false;
      let inBrace = false;
      let braceStart = -1;

      if (ast.type === null) {
        let i = pos;
        let acc = '';
        while (i < str.length) {
          const c = str.charAt(i++);
          if (escaping || c === '\\') {
            escaping = !escaping;
            acc += c;
            continue;
          }
          if (inBrace) {
            if (c === ']' && i !== braceStart + 1) inBrace = false;
            acc += c;
            continue;
          } else if (c === '[') {
            inBrace = true;
            braceStart = i;
            acc += c;
            continue;
          }
          if (!opt.noext && isExtglobType(c) && str.charAt(i) === '(') {
            ast.push(acc);
            acc = '';
            const ext = new AST(c, ast);
            i = AST.parseAST(str, ext, i, opt);
            ast.push(ext);
            continue;
          }
          acc += c;
        }
        ast.push(acc);
        return i;
      }

      let i = pos + 1;
      let part = new AST(null, ast);
      const parts = [];
      let acc = '';
      while (i < str.length) {
        const c = str.charAt(i++);
        if (escaping || c === '\\') {
          escaping = !escaping;
          acc += c;
          continue;
        }
        if (isExtglobType(c) && str.charAt(i) === '(') {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          const ext = new AST(c, part);
          part.push(ext);
          i = AST.parseAST(str, ext, i, opt);
          continue;
        }
        if (c === '|') {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          parts.push(part);
          part = new AST(null, ast);
          continue;
        }
        if (c === ')') {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          ast.push(...parts, part);
          return i;
        }
        acc += c;
      }
      ast.type = null;
      ast.parts = [str.substring(pos - 1)];
      return i;
    }

    static fromGlob(pattern, options = {}) {
      const ast = new AST(null, undefined, options);
      AST.parseAST(pattern, ast, 0, options);
      return ast;
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1391: the same parser refusing to recurse past a configured extglob recursion ceiling"
  lang javascript
  file src/ast.ts
  reject_review code.NestedExtglobExpansionWithoutDepthLimit
  code
  ```
  const types = new Set(['!', '?', '+', '*', '@']);
  const isExtglobType = (c) => types.has(c);

  export class AST {
    constructor(type, parent, options = {}) {
      this.type = type;
      this.parts = [];
      this.parent = parent;
    }

    push(...parts) {
      for (const p of parts) {
        if (p === '') continue;
        this.parts.push(p);
      }
    }

    static parseAST(str, ast, pos, opt, extDepth) {
      const maxDepth = opt.maxExtglobRecursion ?? 2;
      let escaping = false;
      let inBrace = false;
      let braceStart = -1;

      if (ast.type === null) {
        let i = pos;
        let acc = '';
        while (i < str.length) {
          const c = str.charAt(i++);
          if (escaping || c === '\\') {
            escaping = !escaping;
            acc += c;
            continue;
          }
          if (inBrace) {
            if (c === ']' && i !== braceStart + 1) inBrace = false;
            acc += c;
            continue;
          } else if (c === '[') {
            inBrace = true;
            braceStart = i;
            acc += c;
            continue;
          }
          const doRecurse =
            !opt.noext &&
            isExtglobType(c) &&
            str.charAt(i) === '(' &&
            extDepth <= maxDepth;
          if (doRecurse) {
            ast.push(acc);
            acc = '';
            const ext = new AST(c, ast);
            i = AST.parseAST(str, ext, i, opt, extDepth + 1);
            ast.push(ext);
            continue;
          }
          acc += c;
        }
        ast.push(acc);
        return i;
      }

      let i = pos + 1;
      let part = new AST(null, ast);
      const parts = [];
      let acc = '';
      while (i < str.length) {
        const c = str.charAt(i++);
        if (escaping || c === '\\') {
          escaping = !escaping;
          acc += c;
          continue;
        }
        const doRecurse =
          isExtglobType(c) && str.charAt(i) === '(' && extDepth <= maxDepth;
        if (doRecurse) {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          const ext = new AST(c, part);
          part.push(ext);
          i = AST.parseAST(str, ext, i, opt, extDepth + 1);
          continue;
        }
        if (c === '|') {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          parts.push(part);
          part = new AST(null, ast);
          continue;
        }
        if (c === ')') {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          ast.push(...parts, part);
          return i;
        }
        acc += c;
      }
      ast.type = null;
      ast.parts = [str.substring(pos - 1)];
      return i;
    }

    static fromGlob(pattern, options = {}) {
      const ast = new AST(null, undefined, options);
      AST.parseAST(pattern, ast, 0, options, 0);
      return ast;
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1391: the same parser bounding nesting through a differently named local ceiling"
  lang javascript
  file src/ast.ts
  reject_review code.NestedExtglobExpansionWithoutDepthLimit
  code
  ```
  const types = new Set(['!', '?', '+', '*', '@']);
  const isExtglobType = (c) => types.has(c);

  export class AST {
    constructor(type, parent, options = {}) {
      this.type = type;
      this.parts = [];
      this.parent = parent;
    }

    push(...parts) {
      for (const p of parts) {
        if (p === '') continue;
        this.parts.push(p);
      }
    }

    static parseAST(str, ast, pos, opt, nestingDepth) {
      const maxNesting = 2;
      let escaping = false;

      if (ast.type === null) {
        let i = pos;
        let acc = '';
        while (i < str.length) {
          const c = str.charAt(i++);
          if (escaping || c === '\\') {
            escaping = !escaping;
            acc += c;
            continue;
          }
          if (
            !opt.noext &&
            isExtglobType(c) &&
            str.charAt(i) === '(' &&
            nestingDepth < maxNesting
          ) {
            ast.push(acc);
            acc = '';
            const ext = new AST(c, ast);
            i = AST.parseAST(str, ext, i, opt, nestingDepth + 1);
            ast.push(ext);
            continue;
          }
          acc += c;
        }
        ast.push(acc);
        return i;
      }

      let i = pos + 1;
      let part = new AST(null, ast);
      const parts = [];
      let acc = '';
      while (i < str.length) {
        const c = str.charAt(i++);
        if (
          isExtglobType(c) &&
          str.charAt(i) === '(' &&
          nestingDepth < maxNesting
        ) {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          const ext = new AST(c, part);
          part.push(ext);
          i = AST.parseAST(str, ext, i, opt, nestingDepth + 1);
          continue;
        }
        if (c === '|') {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          parts.push(part);
          part = new AST(null, ast);
          continue;
        }
        if (c === ')') {
          part.push(acc);
          acc = '';
          ast.push(...parts, part);
          return i;
        }
        acc += c;
      }
      return i;
    }

    static fromGlob(pattern, options = {}) {
      const ast = new AST(null, undefined, options);
      AST.parseAST(pattern, ast, 0, options, 0);
      return ast;
    }
  }
  ```
